Great question! When a class implements multiple interfaces that contain default methods with the same signature,
Java requires the class to override the conflicting method to resolve the ambiguity. This avoids confusion about which method to use.

✅ Correct way to resolve the conflict:
You must override the display() method in MyClass and explicitly choose which interface's default method to call (if desired).

✅ Example: Explicitly calling a specific interface's method
java
Copy
Edit
interface A {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from A");
    }
}

interface B {
    default void display() {
        System.out.println("Display from B");
    }
}

class MyClass implements A, B {
    @Override
    public void display() {
        // Option 1: Choose one interface to call
        A.super.display();  // or B.super.display();

        // Option 2: Add custom logic
        System.out.println("MyClass resolving display");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass obj = new MyClass();
        obj.display();
    }
}
🔑 Notes:
A.super.display() means: Call the default method from interface A.

You can only use InterfaceName.super.method() syntax inside the implementing class, not from outside.